steps.body:
`JSON.parse` is typed to return `any`, so anything you read from its result compiles, a misspelled property included:

```typescript
const config = JSON.parse('{"retries": 3}');
//    ^? const config: any
console.log(config.retires); // undefined
```

Stop the `any` where it starts. Put `JSON.parse` in a function whose return type is `unknown`, so every caller has to narrow the value before using it. Text that isn't JSON makes `JSON.parse` throw a `SyntaxError`, so the same function is the place to catch that and return a Result instead:

```typescript types
type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };
```

Most JSON holds objects, so one guard saves you from repeating the first checks every time:

```typescript
function isRecord(x: unknown): x is Record<string, unknown> {
  return typeof x === "object" && x !== null && !Array.isArray(x);
}
const data: unknown = JSON.parse('{"port": 8080}');
if (isRecord(data)) {
  const port = data.port;
  //    ^? const port: unknown
  console.log(port); // 8080
}
```

After `isRecord`, every property you read is `unknown` again, ready for its own check: `typeof` narrows it to a primitive, and `===` narrows it to a literal like `"dark"`.

examples.code:
type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };

function parseJson(text: string): Result<unknown, string> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(text) };
  } catch (err) {
    return { ok: false, error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) };
  }
}

for (const text of ['{"retries": 3}', "[1, 2", "null"]) {
  const result = parseJson(text);
  if (result.ok) {
    const value = result.value;
    //    ^? const value: unknown
    console.log(`ok: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
  } else {
    console.log(`error: ${result.error}`);
  }
}

